; The few functions every compiled program needs and WebAssembly does not have.
;
; The backend's values are 64-bit integers, and a string, a byte string or a variant is
; the address of words in linear memory. Making one, copying one and comparing two takes
; code that is not any function of the program being compiled, so it is written here once,
; as instructions, and put at the front of every module. There are seven:
;
;   alloc(n)            a block of n bytes, rounded up to a word, from a bump pointer;
;                       it grows the memory when the pointer passes the end
;   new(len)            a Str or Bytes of that length, its length word written
;   concat(a, b)        a new one holding a then b
;   eq(a, b)            1 when both have the same bytes, else 0
;   at(b, i)            `(Some byte)` or `(None)`, which is `bytes.at`
;   slice(b, from, to)  `bytes.slice`: clamped, and empty when from reaches to
;   of(n)               a one-byte string of n's low byte
;
; A function's index among the module's own functions is fixed here: these come first and the
; program's own functions follow. Imports are numbered before all of them; the encoder adds
; their count to every call, so nothing here depends on how many there are. `first_user` is the first index
; the backend may give out.
;
; Two things differ per program and so are not fixed: which cell is `(None)`, and what id
; `(Some ...)` has. They are the program's own numbering of its variants, so the lowering
; passes them in.
;
; Text and number conversion that is easier to write in Witness than in instructions is in
; compiler/rtlib.wtn, which is compiled along with the program like any other source.
;
;   witness test compiler/runtime.wtn
